ience.nasa.gov/sun/sunspots/">View NASA source record</a></p></article><article><h2>Flare</h2><figure class=""><img src="assets/images/flare.jpg" alt="A long filament erupts from the Sun." loading="lazy"><figcaption>A solar eruption associated with a flare, August 31, 2012. SDO/AIA; extreme ultraviolet shown in assigned color. Credit: NASA/SDO. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/magnificent-outburst/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure><p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/magnificent-outburst/">View NASA source record</a></p></article><article><h2>Aurora</h2><figure class=""><img src="assets/images/aurora.jpg" alt="Green and purple aurora above the curved Earth at night." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Aurora above Earth, September 7, 2026; photographed from the ISS. Credit: NASA / ISS Crew Earth Observations Facility. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/purple-haze-aurora/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure><p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/purple-haze-aurora/">View NASA source record</a></p></article><article><h2>Parker</h2><figure class=""><img src="assets/images/parker.png" alt="An artist’s illustration of Parker Solar Probe near the Sun." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Parker Solar Probe, artist’s illustration. Credit: NASA/APL. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/parker-solar-probe/nasas-parker-solar-probe-makes-history-with-closest-pass-to-sun/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure><p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/parker-solar-probe/nasas-parker-solar-probe-makes-history-with-closest-pass-to-sun/">View NASA source record</a></p></article><h2>Teaching diagrams</h2><p>Original diagrams are simplified and not to scale. Science reference: <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/">NASA Sun facts</a>. Full image and use details are in <a href="SOURCES-AND-IMAGE-CREDITS.md">the source manifest</a>.</p></main></body></html>